---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import ResearchProject from '../components/ResearchProject.astro';
import Lightbox from '../components/Lightbox.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Research"
  description="Research projects by Md Jubier Ahammed on disaster risk, social vulnerability, remote sensing, and land cover classification."
>
  <PageHeader title="Research" eyebrow="Projects">
    <p>
      My M.S. thesis, disaster risk research, and other work in progress. Published papers and manuscripts
      under review are listed on the <a href="/publications/">Publications</a> page.
    </p>
  </PageHeader>

  <section id="thesis" class="group" aria-labelledby="thesis-heading">
    <h2 id="thesis-heading">M.S. Thesis</h2>

    <ResearchProject
      id="ms-thesis"
      title="Transferability of a Google Earth Engine Land Cover Classification Framework Between the Ganges and Mississippi Deltas"
      status="M.S. thesis · In progress · Advisor: Dr. Chao Xu"
      methods="Google Earth Engine, Landsat, seasonal eight-class Random Forest classification, spatially blocked validation, NLCD crosswalk, transition analysis"
    >
      <p>
        Delta wetlands change on two timescales at once. Over decades, sediment starvation, subsidence, embankment,
        and land conversion reshape the delta plain; within a single year, monsoon flooding, tidal inundation, and
        crop cycles move the boundary between water, wetland, and farmland across large areas and then move it back.
        Separating seasonal fluctuation from long-term change requires classifications that are explicitly seasonal
        as well as multi-decadal, and that is the approach this thesis adopts.
      </p>
      <p>
        The study proceeds in two stages. Stage one establishes a seasonal eight-class Random Forest classification
        framework for the Ganges Delta, covering 2014 to 2026, and analyses the resulting long-term dynamics. The
        framework is trained on reference samples derived through visual interpretation of high-resolution imagery
        and evaluated under spatially blocked validation. My contribution to this stage includes reference-sample
        collection, code implementation and testing, quality control, result organisation, interpretation, and
        documentation. Stage two, the principal original contribution, adapts and improves that framework for the
        Mississippi Delta, where the climate is temperate rather than monsoonal, the wetland vegetation and sediment
        regimes differ, and the reference data come from the National Land Cover Database rather than manual
        interpretation. This stage reconsiders the classification scheme against what Mississippi wetland conditions
        can support, redefines the seasonal windows around the local hydrological year, repeats feature selection,
        and recalibrates the model, then evaluates class by class what transferred, what required regional
        modification, and what could not be separated in the second region. The final analysis produces long-term
        land-cover records and transition matrices for both deltas and compares their dynamics in relation to their
        contrasting geomorphic, hydrological, ecological, and human settings.
      </p>
      <p class="no-justify"><strong>Research questions</strong></p>
      <ol class="questions">
        <li>
          How can the eight-class classification framework developed for the Ganges Delta be adapted to the different
          land-cover, spectral, hydrological, and reference-data conditions of the Mississippi Delta?
        </li>
        <li>
          Which spectral features, temporal statistics, and class-specific rules are transferable between the two
          deltas, and which require regional modification to achieve acceptable classification accuracy?
        </li>
        <li>
          How have the extent, spatial distribution, and transitions of the eight land-cover classes changed in the
          Ganges and Mississippi Deltas over the study period?
        </li>
        <li>
          How are the similarities and differences in land-cover dynamics between the two deltas related to their
          contrasting geomorphic, hydrological, ecological, and human settings?
        </li>
      </ol>
    </ResearchProject>
  </section>

  <section id="disaster-risk" class="group" aria-labelledby="disaster-risk-heading">
    <h2 id="disaster-risk-heading">Disaster Risk &amp; Social Vulnerability</h2>

    <ResearchProject
      id="uri-nighttime-lights"
      title="What Daily Nighttime Lights Can and Cannot Show About Power Outage and Recovery"
      status="Winter Storm Uri, Harris County, TX · Published in ISPRS International Journal of Geo-Information, 2026"
      methods="R, VIIRS Black Marble daily nighttime lights, recovery detection workflows, controlled smoothing/persistence experiment, SVI comparison against an ordinary-night baseline"
      links={[
        { label: 'Read the paper', href: 'https://doi.org/10.3390/ijgi15110491' },
        { label: '2nd Place, Graduate Poster, SWAAG 2026', href: '/awards/#swaag-2026' },
      ]}
    >
      <p>
        When the Texas power grid failed during Winter Storm Uri in 2021, satellites watched the lights go out. This
        paper asks how far daily NASA Black Marble nighttime-light data can really go in telling that story. During
        the outage the daily record is thin: usable observations exist for only 16 February, then 19 to 21 February.
        On 16 February, 37% of developed pixels in Harris County were below half their pre-storm brightness, and 92%
        of those with an observed return were lit again by 21 February. But the recovery dates that detector
        workflows produce depend heavily on smoothing and persistence choices, with a median detection delay of 12 to
        15 days in a controlled experiment. The association with social vulnerability could not be distinguished from
        ordinary night-to-night variation, so the paper recommends comparing against an ordinary-night baseline before
        drawing community-level conclusions from a single night.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>
          Presented as a poster at SWAAG 2026 (preprint version, "Spatial Pattern of Post-storm Recovery Revealed by
          Nighttime Light"), where it won 2nd Place in the Graduate Poster competition.
        </em>
      </p>
    </ResearchProject>
  </section>

  <section id="in-preparation" class="group" aria-labelledby="in-preparation-heading">
    <h2 id="in-preparation-heading">In Preparation</h2>

    <ResearchProject
      id="ganges-wetlands"
      title="Tracking Coastal Wetland Dynamics in the Ganges Delta, 1985–2026"
      status="In preparation · with Dr. Chao Xu"
      methods="Google Earth Engine, Landsat, ArcGIS Pro, ArcPy"
    >
      <p>
        A four-decade look at how the Ganges Delta's wetlands have shifted, built on reference samples I
        derived through visual interpretation of high-resolution imagery and digitised in ArcGIS Pro, with a custom
        Python workflow to keep attribute data consistent across editing sessions.
      </p>
    </ResearchProject>

    <ResearchProject
      id="baton-rouge-lst"
      title="Land Surface Temperature and Socioeconomic Disproportionality in Baton Rouge's Industrial Corridor"
      status="Ongoing"
      methods="Google Earth Engine, Random Forest, XGBoost, SHAP/PDP, spatial regression"
    >
      <p>
        Louisiana's industrial corridor runs hotter than its surroundings, but is that heat driven by land cover and
        infrastructure alone, or does it also track who lives there? This two-stage project first uses tree-based
        machine learning, Random Forest, XGBoost, and Gradient Boosting, interpreted through SHAP and partial
        dependence plots, to identify what physically drives land surface temperature across three clean Landsat
        scenes spanning a decade. The second stage tests whether the resulting heat patterns disproportionately burden
        specific socioeconomic groups, using a deliberately conservative regression design that avoids composite
        indices and multicollinear variables.
      </p>
    </ResearchProject>
  </section>

  <Lightbox />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .group + .group {
    margin-top: var(--section-gap);
  }

  .questions {
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .questions li {
    text-align: justify;
    hyphens: auto;
  }

  .questions li + li {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  .group h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.75rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }
</style>
